<ArticleLayout slug="brass-copper-or-stainless-steel-for-hospitality">
  <p>
    Most specification mistakes in hospitality metalware are material mistakes. They are also the
    expensive kind, because they do not show up in week one. They show up in year two, in front of a
    guest, across an entire batch at once.
  </p>

  <h2>The rule that is not negotiable</h2>
  <blockquote>
    Copper and brass are beautiful, and neither belongs against food.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Both react with anything acidic — tomato, citrus, vinegar, wine. The reaction is bad for the food
    and worse for the guest. Every piece of copper or brass we make that holds something edible is
    lined in 304 stainless steel, and there is no cheaper version of that decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a supplier offers unlined copper cookware or drinkware for hospitality use, that is a
    food-safety failure waiting for an audit. It is the single fastest way to judge whether you are
    dealing with a manufacturer or a trader.
  </p>

  <h2>Stainless steel: the default, with one caveat</h2>
  <p>
    Grade 304 is the working standard for food contact. Grade 201 costs about a fifth less and is
    perfectly acceptable in a frame, a leg or a structural part — but it will pit within a season in
    a coastal property or on a chlorinated pool deck.
  </p>
  <p>
    Specify steel wherever the piece lives outdoors, goes through a commercial dishwasher forty times
    a week, or sits in direct sun. Under cool light it can read slightly clinical, which is what the
    other two metals are for.
  </p>

  <h3>A note on PVD</h3>
  <p>
    PVD is a vacuum-deposited coating, not a plating. It is harder than the steel underneath,
    dishwasher-safe and does not tarnish. A PVD-gold charger plate will outlive a gold-plated one
    several times over. For hospitality volume, PVD is almost always the right answer; genuine
    plating is for presentation pieces where a real precious-metal surface matters.
  </p>

  <h2>Brass: for the room that wants to look expensive</h2>
  <p>
    Brass is the only one of the three we can cast, which is why every finial, handle and tripod leg
    in our range is brass even when the body is not. Under the warm light most banquet halls and
    lounges use, brass holds where steel goes flat.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lacquered:</strong> stays bright, needs only a damp cloth. Fails if scoured or dishwashed, then tarnishes in patches.</li>
    <li><strong>Antiqued:</strong> chemically darkened into the recesses. It is in the metal, so it cannot chip — but never polish it, because polishing is what removes it.</li>
    <li><strong>Raw:</strong> develops a patina. Beautiful, and a standing labour cost.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One tell worth knowing: a set of five hundred perfectly identical "antique" brass plates has been
    sprayed, not antiqued. Chemical antiquing varies slightly piece to piece. Spray comes off.
  </p>

  <h2>Copper: the most photographed, the most demanding</h2>
  <p>
    Copper conducts heat away roughly twenty times faster than stainless steel — which is exactly
    wrong for an ice bucket, and exactly why it gets lined. What copper is genuinely good at is
    looking like nothing else in a room under low light.
  </p>
  <p>
    Copper is also hand-raised rather than machine-spun on most shapes. An 18-inch oval platter takes
    a craftsman about five hours, and no two are identical. That is a feature in a restaurant and a
    problem in a spec that demands uniformity, so decide which you are buying.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing by room</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Specify</th><th>Because</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Poolside, terrace, any direct sun</td><td>304 steel or PVD</td><td>Lacquered brass chalks under UV; plating fails in salt air.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Banquet hall, warm light</td><td>Brass or copper</td><td>Warm metals hold under 2700K where steel reads grey.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bar and lounge, low light</td><td>Copper, antique brass</td><td>Handled at eye level; warm metal flatters at close range.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Daily breakfast buffet</td><td>Hammered steel</td><td>Highest wash cycle of anything in the building.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fine dining table</td><td>Any, but match the light</td><td>Mirror under cool light, satin or antique under warm.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Hammered or smooth</h2>
  <p>
    For daily service, hammered — almost always. A hand-hammered face scatters light, and service
    scuffing scatters light the same way, so marks disappear into the surface instead of sitting on
    it. Smooth and mirror finishes are for pieces that are seen more than they are used.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every finish we offer, and what each one does over time, is set out on
    <a href="/materials-and-finishes">the materials and finishes page</a>. If you are matching
    something you already own, send us the physical piece rather than a photograph — finish is about
    how a surface handles light, and a photograph cannot carry that.
  </p>
